{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1788594163011788001,"compiled_state":{"alias":"gigantic-faulty-rabbit","assignee":null,"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":1,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"df213c819bc73384aa5e10fb19263d1c384c4d99502cc98906bb68acf8a9d63d","event_uuid":"054704ab-1c29-4592-905e-44c9a5ca38bf","position":{"commit_sha":"b5eebf4a662bf95281bf4c67c38f8c9536b80f3b","position":"1788590567613373001-054704ab-1c29-4592-905e-44c9a5ca38bf"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[],"created_at":1788590567613373001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[],"description":"## The near-miss\n\nAn agent preparing Gerrit change 2665 staged its commit message at the fixed path\n`/tmp/commitmsg.txt`. A concurrent session working in `/Users/joeoakhart/windows-lock-s1` wrote to\n**the same path**. The agent's `git commit -F /tmp/commitmsg.txt` then picked up the OTHER\nsession's message body — \"Give the store write lock a Windows leg\" — and attached it to its own,\ncompletely unrelated diff (EBS mount assertions in `infra/terraform/user_data.sh`).\n\nIt was caught only because the agent read the commit back before pushing. It recovered with\n`make amend-msg` (Change-Id preserved, so no duplicate change) and switched to a `mktemp -d` path.\n\n## Why this is worth a ticket rather than a shrug\n\nHad it not read the commit back, a Gerrit change would have gone up for review carrying a **false\ndescription of what it does**. Reviewers, the `rebar-ticket:` trailer audit, `git log`, and every\nfuture bisect would all have agreed with each other and all been wrong. That is the same defect\nclass this session has spent the night filing — an artefact asserting something that was never\ntrue — except lodged in the permanent commit record rather than in a check.\n\nThe conditions that produced it are not exotic. This host currently runs **eleven active\nworktrees** across several concurrent agent sessions, all as the same OS user, all with `/tmp`\nshared. A fixed filename under `/tmp` is not a race that might happen; it is a collision waiting\nfor two sessions to reach the same step, which on a busy night is minutes apart.\n\nNote the asymmetry that makes it dangerous: the collision is SILENT. `git commit -F` succeeded,\nexit 0, no warning. Nothing distinguishes \"my message\" from \"someone else's message\" at the point\nof use — only a human or agent reading the result back can tell.\n\n## Scope — this is about guidance and tooling, not one agent\n\nThe agent that hit it did the right things (read back, recovered without a duplicate change,\nswitched to `mktemp -d`, disclosed it prominently). The question is why a fixed `/tmp` path was\nreachable at all:\n\n- this session's own operating convention is a per-job scratch directory precisely for this\n  reason, and that convention lives in the harness prompt rather than anywhere an agent working\n  from repo guidance would find it;\n- `CONTRIBUTING.md` / `AGENTS.md` document `make amend-msg` for message rewrites but say nothing\n  about WHERE to stage a message body;\n- nothing in the repo's tooling refuses or warns on a shared-`/tmp` path.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Repo guidance states plainly that any scratch file — commit message bodies especially — must\n      go in a per-session directory (`mktemp -d`, or the session's own scratch dir), never a fixed\n      path under `/tmp`, because multiple agent sessions share this host as one OS user.\n- [ ] The reasoning is recorded with the guidance, not just the rule: the failure is SILENT and\n      lands in the permanent commit record. A rule without its reason gets optimised away.\n- [ ] Consider whether `make amend-msg` and any commit-message helper should reject a path under\n      `/tmp` that is not session-unique, or at minimum warn. Decide either way and record why —\n      a guard is only worth adding if it cannot itself become noise.\n- [ ] Sweep the repo's scripts and documented workflows for other fixed `/tmp` paths that two\n      concurrent sessions could collide on, and record each with a disposition. Enumerate the\n      result; do not fix only the commit-message case.","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":[],"ticket_id":"919d-7994-3ac7-4d0f","ticket_type":"bug","title":"A fixed /tmp path let one session's commit message land on another session's diff: change 2665 nearly went to review carrying a false description, caught only by reading the commit back","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["054704ab-1c29-4592-905e-44c9a5ca38bf"]},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1788594163011788001,"uuid":"376cd5da-15e8-4508-8b20-ce4eba2f5e00"}